---
BasedOnStyle: Google
DerivePointerAlignment: false
PointerAlignment: Left
LineEnding: LF
InsertNewlineAtEOF: true
# Always put the declaration that follows a `requires` clause on its own line.
# This is needed for the `CPP_template` macros below; with `SingleLine`, the
# declaration often directly follows the closing parenthesis of a multi-line
# clause.
#
# CAVEAT: This also applies to the (C++20-only) trailing `requires` clauses of
# functions. clang-format 18 then always puts the `= delete`, `= default`, or
# the opening brace of the function body on a separate line. None of the other
# values of `RequiresClausePosition` avoids this without breaking the
# formatting of the `CPP_template` macros. Where this is too ugly (e.g. for the
# special member functions in `IdTable.h`), use `// clang-format off`.
RequiresClausePosition: WithPreceding
IndentRequiresClause: false
AttributeMacros: [CPP_member, CPP_auto_member, CPP_fun, CPP_auto_fun]
# Teach clang-format the `CPP_template(params...)(requires ...)` macros (and
# their variants), such that the `requires` clause is formatted as an actual
# `requires` clause and the declaration starts on its own line, for example:
#
#   CPP_template_def(typename T)(
#       requires SomeConcept<T> && OtherConcept<T>)
#   void Foo::f(T t) {
#
# How this works: clang-format formats the code after expanding the macros
# defined here, but it only knows the first parenthesized argument list of a
# macro. The `(requires ...)` part is ordinary code that directly follows the
# macro call. That part is laid out once in the expanded code and keeps that
# layout when mapped back to the original code. We therefore expand
# `CPP_template(params...)` to
#
#   template <class CppTemplateT> requires const_cast<params...>
#
# The `template <...>` makes the line a template declaration (with a line break
# after it). The `requires const_cast<params...>` then makes the following
# `(requires ...)` the parenthesized operand of a `requires` clause (a cast is
# the only construct that clang-format accepts directly before the parentheses
# of a constraint). As the parameters themselves are part of that line, the
# `(requires ...)` part is laid out only a few columns further to the right than
# in the original code, so the result stays within the column limit.
#
# The macros are not variadic for clang-format, so there is one definition per
# number of macro arguments (note that commas inside of `<...>` also separate
# macro arguments). For now, only the single-argument calls of the macros below
# are expanded. The plain `CPP_template` macro and calls with more arguments
# will follow in subsequent changes.
#
# NOTE: Prefer combining constraints with `&&` over the `CPP_and` macro (which
# the existing code uses in many places). `CPP_and` gives slightly better error
# messages for violated constraints in C++17 mode, but defining it as a macro
# here makes the continuation lines inconsistent, and in combination with
# `CPP_and(...)` clang-format 18 crashes or runs into an infinite loop. Use
# `CPP_and` temporarily when debugging such an error.
Macros:
  - 'CPP_template_2(a)=template <class CppTemplateT> requires const_cast<a>'
  - 'CPP_template_def(a)=template <class CppTemplateT> requires const_cast<a>'
  - 'CPP_template_out_def(a)=template <class CppTemplateT> requires const_cast<a>'
  - 'CPP_variadic_template(a)=template <class CppTemplateT> requires const_cast<a>'
  - 'CPP_variadic_template_def(a)=template <class CppTemplateT> requires const_cast<a>'
  - 'CPP_class_template(a)=template <class CppTemplateT> requires const_cast<a>'
...
